rows.en:
Hmm, so you get it?
Well, Kawahara gradually started to find that kind of thing painful.
He'd smile and smile at his teachers and friends, while sticking his tongue out at them inside.{p}

It was Kawahara's textbook.
Remember, I said he'd lent his chemistry textbook to someone?
That one.{p}

...And then, before chemistry class started, the teacher came out with this.
One of the question sheets for the next exam had gone missing.
'I don't want to believe anyone stole it, but...' That was the gist, apparently.{p}

...He could just secretly throw away whatever was stuck in the textbook.
Whatever it was, if Koizumi put it there, it couldn't be anything good.
That's what he thought.{p}

The teacher came closer, one step at a time.
Kawahara backed away.
Forgetting that he was standing at the top of the stairs.{p}

Fukuzawa stood there trembling, still pointing behind me.
What on earth was wrong?
I fearfully turned around.{p}

They say anyone who sees it is sure to meet with misfortune...
What kind of misfortune?
Well, that part I don't know.{p}

Okay, that's the end of my story.
Let's head back to the club room.{p}Maybe the seventh one's already shown up, you know?
Right, {b}...?{p}

Right?
I can't stand people like that either.
If you've got something to say, you should just come out and say it, right?{p}

...I'll tell the truth after all.
That's what Kawahara decided.
If there really was a question sheet in there, it'd be better not to keep quiet about it.{p}
